STEP 1.1.1 condition 3 — mini-I-3 C++ sanitizer/oracle extension: EVIDENCE
=========================================================================
Image X' (:phase1 = d45e33b0). Probe: scripts/corpus/sanitizer/{cpp_probe.pyx,
run_cpp_sanitizer_probe.sh}. C++ Cython unit (libcpp.vector + 3 C++ throws via except+),
built g++-13 -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -ffp-contract=fast.

[A] FAILURE FOUND (Phase-0 sanitizer runtime env, C path only): a C++ throw aborts ASan:
    "AddressSanitizer: CHECK failed asan_interceptors.cpp:458 real___cxa_throw != 0"
    Cause: ASan loaded via LD_PRELOAD into a non-instrumented python3.12; at interceptor
    init libstdc++ is not yet present, so ASan's __cxa_throw interceptor never binds the real
    symbol -> first C++ exception unwind CHECK-fails. (Allocation path use_vector ran fine;
    the failure is SPECIFICALLY C++ exception unwinding.)

[B] FIX (runtime-env, NOT a suppression line): add libstdc++.so.6 to LD_PRELOAD AHEAD of the
    throw so __cxa_throw exists when ASan binds interceptors:
      LD_PRELOAD="$(gcc-13 -print-file-name=libasan.so):$(g++-13 -print-file-name=libstdc++.so.6)"
    Result with fix:
      RUN detect_leaks=0 (operational / feasibility gate): exit=0, CLEAN.
        use_vector(1_000_000) sum = 499999500000  (correct; ASan/UBSan no report)
      RUN detect_leaks=1 (leak-audit): SUMMARY 769 byte(s) in 2 allocs — ALL CPython
        (PyMem_Malloc / PyModule_ExecDef / __Pyx_FetchCommonTypeFromSpec), i.e. the SAME
        by-design teardown leaks lsan_cpython.supp + detect_leaks=0 already cover.
        *** NO libstdc++ leak appeared for this archetype (vector + exceptions). ***

[C] C++ -> Python EXCEPTION-IDENTITY (oracle §3.1) — deterministic via Cython except+:
      std::out_of_range     -> builtins.IndexError
      std::length_error     -> builtins.RuntimeError   (no specific map -> catch-all RuntimeError)
      std::invalid_argument -> builtins.ValueError
    The oracle compares the TRANSLATED Python exception type (canonical module.QualName,
    subclass never matches), so the existing §3.1 exception-identity comparator works UNCHANGED
    on C++ units: the "raised exception" it sees is the deterministically-mapped Python type.

CONCLUSION (evidence over anticipated mechanism): the C++ enablement is (1) a build-profile
addition (g++-13 --cplus + the same ASan/UBSan flags) and (2) a RUNTIME-ENV change (libstdc++
preload, justified by [A]/[B]) — NOT new suppression-file lines. No libstdc++ leak/UBSan
suppression was needed for the tested archetype; if a SPECIFIC corpus C++ unit surfaces a
libstdc++ teardown leak in leak-audit, a justified `leak:` line is added THEN (evidence-grounded,
per the original I-3 discipline), not speculatively.

[D] COMPREHENSIVE RIG AUDIT (non-vacuous; human conditions 2+3). Probe
    scripts/corpus/sanitizer/cpp_rig_probe.pyx + rig_audit.sh, image X'. Results:
    (a) CLEAN operational (detect_leaks=0): exit=0, clean_stl=1999000, no ASan/UBSan FP.
    (b) exception map: out_of_range->IndexError, length_error->RuntimeError, invalid_argument->ValueError.
    (c) NON-VACUITY — rig CATCHES each real C++ bug (more than throws, per condition 2):
          bug_oob  -> ASan heap-buffer-overflow      (STL vector bounds)   CAUGHT (exit 1)
          bug_uaf  -> ASan heap-use-after-free        (iterator invalidation via realloc) CAUGHT
          bug_vptr -> UBSan runtime error (vptr)      (wrong dynamic type / RTTI) CAUGHT
    (d) LEAK POLICY (condition 3) — RESOLVED EMPIRICALLY (better than the detect_leaks=0 fallback):
          detect_leaks=1 + LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=lsan_cpython.supp:
            clean unit  -> 0 unsuppressed leaks (CPython fully suppressed; exit 0)
            stl_leak()  -> LSan REPORTS the leak (Direct leak, operator new in _stl_leak) -> exit 1
          => STL container leaks ARE directly detectable in a dedicated C++ leak-AUDIT pass.
    PRODUCTION POLICY (documented in SANITIZER_POLICY.md): the FEASIBILITY GATE keeps detect_leaks=0
